### Increasing max-open files for beanstalkd

- Published: 2015-02-28
- Modified: 2021-07-16
- URL: https://rollbar.com/blog/increasing-max-open-files-for-beanstalkd/
- Categories: Code Tutorials
- Tags: behind-the-scenes

Quick tip: If you are running out of file descriptors in your Beanstalkd process, use /etc/default/beanstalkd to set the ulimit before the init script starts the process. e. g. # file: /etc/default/beanstalkd BEANSTALKD_LISTEN_ADDR=127. 0. 0. 1 BEANSTALKD_LISTEN_PORT=11300 START=yes BEANSTALKD_EXTRA="-b /var/lib/beanstalkd -f 1" # Should match your /etc/security/limits. conf settings ulimit -n 100000 Lots of resources online tell you to update your /etc/security/limits. conf and /etc/pam. d/common-session* settings to increase your maximum number of available file descriptors. However, the default beanstalkd installation on Ubuntu 12. 04+ uses an init script that starts the daemon process using start-stop-daemon which does not use your system settings when setting the processes ulimits. Just add this line to your defaults and you're good to go!

